Every `+=` on a string allocates a new string and copies both operands, so a loop doing n concatenations allocates O(n) strings and copies O(n^2) characters. `StringBuilder` mutates one growable buffer instead, giving O(n) copying amortised.

The part worth knowing for an interview is **when this advice does not apply**:

- A fixed, small number of concatenations in one expression (`a + b + c + d`) compiles to a single `String.Concat` call that allocates once. Rewriting that as a `StringBuilder` makes it slower and uglier.
- Concatenating string *literals* is folded at compile time and costs nothing at runtime.
- For a known set of parts, `string.Join` or interpolation is clearer than a builder and about as fast.

So the rule is not "never use +", it is "never use + in a loop whose length you do not control".

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Text;

public static class Program
{
    const int N = 20000;

    public static void Main()
    {
        // --- naive concatenation in a loop ---
        var before = GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes(precise: true);
        var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
        string s = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) s += "x";
        var naiveMs = sw.Elapsed.TotalMilliseconds;
        var naiveBytes = GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes(precise: true) - before;

        // --- StringBuilder ---
        before = GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes(precise: true);
        sw.Restart();
        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) sb.Append('x');
        var built = sb.ToString();
        var builderMs = sw.Elapsed.TotalMilliseconds;
        var builderBytes = GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes(precise: true) - before;

        Console.WriteLine($"both produced the same string : {s == built} (length {s.Length})");
        Console.WriteLine();

        // Ratios, not raw timings - raw numbers would differ on every machine.
        Console.WriteLine($"concat allocated more bytes than builder : {naiveBytes > builderBytes}");
        // The exact ratio drifts between runs, so assert a floor rather than a figure.
        Console.WriteLine($"more than 100x the allocations           : {naiveBytes > builderBytes * 100}");
        Console.WriteLine($"concat was slower                        : {naiveMs > builderMs}");
        Console.WriteLine();

        // The case where StringBuilder is the WRONG answer.
        var parts = new[] { "order", "1041", "acme", "shipped" };
        before = GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes(precise: true);
        var joined = parts[0] + "-" + parts[1] + "-" + parts[2] + "-" + parts[3];
        var concatBytes = GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes(precise: true) - before;

        before = GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes(precise: true);
        var sb2 = new StringBuilder();
        sb2.Append(parts[0]).Append('-').Append(parts[1]).Append('-').Append(parts[2]).Append('-').Append(parts[3]);
        var viaBuilder = sb2.ToString();
        var builder2Bytes = GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes(precise: true) - before;

        Console.WriteLine($"fixed 4-part concat  : '{joined}'");
        Console.WriteLine($"same via builder     : '{viaBuilder}'");
        Console.WriteLine($"builder allocated more for the fixed case : {builder2Bytes > concatBytes}");
        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("a + b + c + d is one String.Concat call, so the builder is pure overhead here.");
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
both produced the same string : True (length 20000)

concat allocated more bytes than builder : True
more than 100x the allocations           : True
concat was slower                        : True

fixed 4-part concat  : 'order-1041-acme-shipped'
same via builder     : 'order-1041-acme-shipped'
builder allocated more for the fixed case : True

a + b + c + d is one String.Concat call, so the builder is pure overhead here.
```

Note the program reports a *ratio* and boolean comparisons rather than millisecond figures. A single unwarmed `Stopwatch` loop is not a benchmark - for real numbers use BenchmarkDotNet, which handles warm-up, multiple iterations and statistics. The allocation difference, measured with `GC.GetTotalAllocatedBytes`, is the robust signal here.

`List<T>.Contains` is a linear scan - O(n). Calling it inside a loop over m items is O(n*m). A `HashSet<T>` lookup is O(1) on average, making the same work O(n + m).

The honest answer includes the threshold: **for small collections the list wins.** Building a hash set costs an allocation and a hash per element, and for a handful of items a linear scan over contiguous memory is faster than hashing - it is cache-friendly and has no indirection. The crossover is usually somewhere in the low tens of elements, which is why this is not a blanket rule.

What makes it a real bug is when n is unbounded or grows with data. Ten thousand against ten thousand is a hundred million comparisons; the same work with a set is twenty thousand operations.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public static class Program
{
    static (long Comparisons, int Found) WithList(List<int> haystack, List<int> needles)
    {
        long comparisons = 0;
        int found = 0;
        foreach (var needle in needles)
        {
            // Count what Contains does internally: a scan until it matches.
            for (int i = 0; i < haystack.Count; i++)
            {
                comparisons++;
                if (haystack[i] == needle) { found++; break; }
            }
        }
        return (comparisons, found);
    }

    static (long Lookups, int Found) WithSet(HashSet<int> haystack, List<int> needles)
    {
        long lookups = 0;
        int found = 0;
        foreach (var needle in needles)
        {
            lookups++;
            if (haystack.Contains(needle)) found++;
        }
        return (lookups, found);
    }

    static void Compare(int size)
    {
        // Half the needles are present, half are not - misses are the worst case
        // for the list, because a miss scans the entire collection.
        var haystack = Enumerable.Range(0, size).ToList();
        var needles = Enumerable.Range(size / 2, size).ToList();
        var set = new HashSet<int>(haystack);

        var list = WithList(haystack, needles);
        var hash = WithSet(set, needles);

        // No micro-timings here: at these sizes a single Stopwatch reading is noise.
        // Comparison counts are exact and make the same point far more strongly.
        Console.WriteLine($"  n={size,-6} list comparisons={list.Comparisons,-12:n0} set lookups={hash.Lookups,-8:n0} " +
                          $"same answer={list.Found == hash.Found,-5} ratio={(double)list.Comparisons / hash.Lookups,8:n0}:1");
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("matching two collections, half the lookups miss:");
        foreach (var size in new[] { 10, 100, 1000, 5000 })
            Compare(size);

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("the work grows quadratically for the list and linearly for the set:");
        long previous = 0;
        foreach (var size in new[] { 1000, 2000, 4000 })
        {
            var haystack = Enumerable.Range(0, size).ToList();
            var needles = Enumerable.Range(size / 2, size).ToList();
            var c = WithList(haystack, needles).Comparisons;
            Console.WriteLine($"  n={size,-6} comparisons={c,-14:n0}" +
                              (previous == 0 ? "" : $"  ({(double)c / previous:0.0}x the previous, for 2x the data)"));
            previous = c;
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
